@0    = ~Oh, I really hope everything will be all right!~
@1    = ~My poor brethren...~
@2    = ~I am sorry, Gerrit, but both Muck and Fuller are dead.~
@3    = ~We spoke to Fuller, and he told us about the ritual. You could have been a *little* more elaborate about what you and your brethren tried in there. The abbot had no idea, either!~
@4    = ~We agreed to have a look inside the catacombs. The abbot mentioned you were the only one who made it out. What exactly happened?~
@5    = ~That doesn't sound good. What happened to your brethren?~
@6    = ~I'll leave you to your prayers.~
@7    = ~We... were facing a great evil, and could not tame it. Oh, my poor brethren, if only I knew what happened to them!~
@8    = ~Any way I could help?~
@9    = ~Go with Ilmater's blessing.~
@10   = ~Ilmater bless you for even suggesting it, my friend. We had adventurers help clear the catacombs before, but this time only someone with great fighting prowess should go in search of our brethren. Talk to the abbot; he is the one to decide.~
@11   = ~We-we went into the inner sanctum to do our incantations and to-to chant a blessing to pacify the restless spirits that usually wander the catacombs, when something went horribly wrong and a great evil manifested... We were swamped with skeletons. We tried to fight them, until we realized we had no chance against the numbers, so we tried to escape. It was only outside that I realized that my two brethren did not make it out...~
@12   = ~Muck had the key to the inner sanctum with him, and he and Fuller are still inside, their fate unknown to us... *moans*~
@13   = ~Poor Muck had the key to the inner sanctum with him... Fuller is still inside, his fate unknown to us... *moans*~
@14   = ~And you really don't know what evil has manifested, or where it came from?~
@15   = ~I know enough.~
@16   = ~No, but I was the one who heard what it said. It-it called itself the protector of the restless dead, their "king" even, who commands them... apart from that, I really do not know.~
@17   = ~Thank you for looking inside! Thank you so much. Ilmater shall bless you, and please find Muck and Fuller...~
@18   = ~I... I am *so* sorry! I was so afraid... There is no excuse for not telling you sooner what we did, but I did not lie about not *knowing* what exactly happened. We thought we were chanting a protective blessing!~
@19   = ~Thank you for saving Fuller—and finding poor Muck.~
@20   = ~Thank you for... for finding poor Fuller and Muck.~
@21   = ~You—you banished the great evil we so recklessly awakened.~
@22   = ~Oh no... My poor brethren. Thank you for... for finding them.~
@23   = ~Ilmater be with you for your help.~
@24   = ~Thank you so much for saving Fuller. And... and help cleaning up our mess...~
@25   = ~I need to repent for what I've done...~
@26   = ~Ilmater be blessed! I tried to fight them back and retrieve the scroll, but there are just too many... I apologize; I should start from the beginning. My brethren and I got hold of a scroll with Myrkul's symbol on it. Myrkul is one the previous Gods of the Dead. With all our knowledge, we were able to decipher the words "undead warding" on it and we were convinced that it was a blessing to give protection from undead, thinking it would give the restless dead peace. Instead, we awakened an entity that now declares our presence an intrusion and summons undead to "defend" the catacombs. I tried everything in my power to stop it, but I am too weak to overcome its defenses. The only way is to retrieve the summoning scroll and banish it. The scroll still lies in the inner sanctum.~
@27   = ~Is... is that Muck lying on the ground over there? Oh, my poor brother...~
@28   = ~You could approach the inner sanctum without stirring any more skeletons! What happened?~
@29   = ~We had a... chat with the restless dead in the catacombs.~
@30   = ~Yes, piece of cake. The entity showed itself to me, by the way.~
@31   = ~Whatever. What now?~
@32   = ~I will wait here at the entrance to the inner catacombs and make sure no skeleton makes its way into the mausoleum.~
@33   = ~Please, retrieve the scroll so we can banish the great evil that manifested here.~
@34   = ~Let me get this straight. You used a scroll you didn't properly understand, but you are sure the same scroll will get rid of the problem it created?~
@35   = ~Here is the scroll. Now what?~
@36   = ~I'll come back later.~
@37   = ~Oh, we could *read* it all right. We just didn't *understand* what Myrkul's "protection" actually means. That it would not soothe the undead, but instead summon an entity that actively uses undead to protect the catacombs from "intruders".~
@38   = ~It needs to be used to cast the banishment. Anyone can do it with the scroll. I'll do it if you hand it to me. I'll just need to get my brethren for backing.~
@39   = ~You said you were swamped with skeletons when you tried to approach the inner sanctum—which didn't happen for me. I have a feeling this is something I should do.~
@40   = ~Let me hold on to it for now. I'll think about it.~
@41   = ~Take it. I'll leave it to you to perform the banishment.~
@42   = ~Please, give the scroll to Abbot Guoremor if not to me. Or, if you are inclined, you could use it yourself.~
@43   = ~You said you were swamped with skeletons when you tried to approach the inner sanctum. I have a feeling this is something I should do.~
@44   = ~I am not surprised it's able to do that, seeing how it coordinated its attacks—only that it chose to reveal itself to you, after everything I tried.~
@45   = ~Don't take it personally. I guess it's just a coincidence.~
@46   = ~Maybe it doesn't like being drawn here. It was kind of annoyed about being disturbed.~
@47   = ~Well, I know why, but it's not important now.~
@48   = ~You are right. We need to focus on what is important now.~
@49   = ~I would be ever grateful if you did.~
@50   = ~I'll banish the undead entity now. You get yourself to safety; you have done enough.~
@51   = ~I'll do it. Stay here. I'm sure there will be nasty surprises and I could use the help.~
@52   = ~I am really grateful the abbot sent you.~
@53   = ~Thank you for all you've done. I'll go see the abbot now, to confess what we've done. I'll also take poor Muck along. Your work here is done, helpful adventurer. Please do not disturb the rest of the graves—I think we've had enough restless undead for a day.~
@54   = ~Thank you. Thank you so much. I'll go see the abbot now, to avow to what we've done. I'll also take poor Muck along. Please come into the monastery when you are done here.~
@55   = ~All right. I will be here, having your back.~
@56   = ~You did it! I could feel the power surge going through the catacombs. Well, and all the raised skeletons were a clear indication, too. I apologize for my poor humor.~
@57   = ~Is that so! What exactly did you find?~
@58   = ~A skeleton that proclaimed itself to be the ruler of the dead manifested in the inner sanctum and said curious things about my father.~
@59   = ~There was a talking skeleton in the inner sanctum. It cannot be fought directly; it only sends its minions.~
@60   = ~Thank you so much for your help.~
@61   = ~You look at the symbols on the scroll's page. The symbols arrange themselves into something you can read.~ [bsblank]
@62   = ~You focus on the scroll's magic, ready to reverse the spell that summoned the protector of the restless dead.~
@63   = ~You leave the scroll be.~
@64   = ~You vocalize the banishment spell. While doing so, the scroll crumbles to dust in your hands.~
@65   = ~You look at the symbols on the scroll's page. The symbols start moving, but they do not stop and they do not form anything you could read. It seems you are too far away from the summoned undead you are aiming to banish.~ [bsblank]
@66   = ~And who do we have here?~
@67   = ~You heard my mother.~
@68   = ~I... I can't believe father is dead! Where will the needed gold come from now?~
@69   = ~Hello there! Are you on your way into the mausoleum?~
@70   = ~Mother, look at them. They are not monks from the monastery, and also *surely* do not own a family tomb in the Seatower!~
@71   = ~Mother, look at them. They might be monks, but I've never seen them at the monastery, and they also *surely* do not own a family tomb in the Seatower!~
@72   = ~You are right, my dear—my bad.~
@73   = ~As a matter of fact, I am. I got the key from the abbot and will have a look inside the catacombs.~
@74   = ~Into the what?~
@75   = ~So, this is the entrance to a mausoleum?~
@76   = ~Watch your tone.~
@77   = ~I need to get going.~
@78   = ~Oh, don't worry, good <SIRMAAM>.~
@79   = ~Good day to you.~
@80   = ~I am now on my way into the mausoleum. I got the key from the abbot and will have a look inside the catacombs.~
@81   = ~Is that so! In that case, good <SIRMAAM>, please keep an eye out for my husband, Lord Dlusker. He went inside to pay respects at our family tomb, but it has taken him an awfully long time already and I fear something happened.~
@82   = ~How did your husband get in there? The abbot said it is locked to the public!~
@83   = ~Seems like your husband got himself into trouble. I'll keep an eye out for him.~
@84   = ~I can't promise anything.~
@85   = ~I apologize in case we offended you, <SIRMAAM>.~
@86   = ~It is indeed, and it also contains our family tomb.~
@87   = ~Well, duh—we are not "the public", we *own* one of the tombs, you nitwit.~
@88   = ~Hester, please!—Forgive us, good <SIRMAAM>, we are both quite anxious about Lord Dlusker's whereabouts. The abbot did alert us to problems in the catacombs, but my husband only went into the mausoleum.~
@89   = ~You'd do us a great favor, <SIRMAAM>.~
@90   = ~Hester, please!~
@91   = ~Good <SIRMAAM>, we heard terrifying noises from within the mausoleum. Did you happen to encounter my husband, Lord Dlusker, and can you tell us about his whereabouts?~
@92   = ~I did indeed find him. I am afraid he is dead.~
@93   = ~I'll talk to you later.~
@94   = ~My husband is d... dead?~
@95   = ~See here? Your husband wanted to call on an ancestor, but the magic somehow backfired and turned him into a zombie. We had to kill him.~
@96   = ~He, and your ancestor he tried to revive with a spell. Well, the ancestor was dead already, I figure, but now his apparition your husband summoned is dead, too.~
@97   = ~He... he cast a spell on our family grave? Oh no, the fool, why would he do that!~
@98   = ~What... what is this? Hester, did you know about this?~
@99   = ~Well... Father hoped we would learn about the treasure our ancestor hinted at in his journal, if only we had the chance to talk to him...~
@100  = ~It seems the only treasure you'll get from your ancestor was what he took with him into his tomb.~
@101  = ~Well, inside that grave waited a nasty surprise, I can tell you.~
@102  = ~Well, judging from the sounds coming from within the tomb, *something* in there is very much ready to wander around, I reckon.~
@103  = ~I do not like the sound of that! Did you plunder our family's grave?!~
@104  = ~Of course not!~
@105  = ~Well, maybe I should have, considering your impertinent behavior!~
@106  = ~(lie) Of course not!~
@107  = ~I took a necklace out, but of course it is yours.~
@108  = ~I took a necklace out, but it's gone now. Sorry.~
@109  = ~Well, I'm glad I did, considering your impertinent behavior!~
@110  = ~Young man, you need to watch your tone—this instant.~
@111  = ~Please, good <SIRMAAM>, would you look into our family's tomb and make sure everything is all right?~
@112  = ~We are so grateful for your help. Please, I beg you to hand us whatever you found inside the grave of our ancestors. We are in dire need of financial support, and with my husband gone, I... I... *moans*~
@113  = ~I see my husbands journal on you—I'll take this right out of your hands, and please forgive me for being so bold.~
@114  = ~If there was anything magical on my husband, please feel free to keep it—as a reward of sorts. With my husband gone, I do not have any use for it, we can't sell it without everyone knowing about our dire situation—and I really, really do not want to possess anything that led to this disaster.~
@115  = ~Of course. I didn't touch the tomb's contents, it's all still there.~
@116  = ~Of course. Here is the family necklace from the tomb.~
@117  = ~Let me come back soon with everything I could find.~
@118  = ~Good lady, I fought a zombie *and* a wraith in your name. I guess a little compensation is more than justified. I'll keep whatever treasure there was.~
@119  = ~Thank you. Thank you *so* much. I hope whatever my husband had on him will be enough to compensate you for your efforts—and to buy your silence about what happened here.~
@120  = ~Oh no—I... I see your point, but this is most unfortunate. Thank you, <SIRMAAM>, nonetheless. You had compensation for your efforts enough, I would wager. Oh, what has this world become.~
@121  = ~Come, Hester. Let us close this chapter as soon as possible.~
@122  = ~The bed is freshly made and ready to use.~
@123  = ~You fold it down and put yourself to bed for a full night's sleep (8 hours).~
@124  = ~You spend the time until midnight.~
@125  = ~You spend the time until eleven at night.~
@126  = ~You spend the time until midday.~
@127  = ~You spend the time until eleven at noon.~
@128  = ~You spend the time until ten at noon.~
@129  = ~You spend the time until dawn starts (6 am).~
@130  = ~You spend the time until dawn ends (7 am).~
@131  = ~You spend the time until dusk starts (9 pm).~
@132  = ~You spend the time until dusk ends (10 pm).~
@133  = ~You leave the bed be.~
